The set of all possible outcomes for a random experiment is called the sample space \Omega.

Tossing a coin: \Omega = \{H,T\}

Tossing a coin twice: \Omega = \{HH,HT,TH,TT\}

Taking EEE 5544 and getting a grade:

\Omega = \{A,A-,B+,B,B-,C+,C,D,F\}

Picking an integer at random:

\Omega = \{\ldots,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,\ldots\}

Checking the temperature (in K) at a random location:

\Omega = (0,\infty)

Finite \Omega: Examples 1, 2, and 3

Countably infinite \Omega: Example 4

Discrete \Omega: Examples 1–4

Continuous (uncountably infinite) \Omega: Example 5
